#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de3fa2fa-304c-583a-8c73-a5ff1db972e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Rectangular Region Onto a Given One"}] :question/id #uuid "87c2cf96-8772-5b28-b6d8-a5616b38543f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12337" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad\ny=7x, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $\\Delta$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T} : (u,v) \\to \\Big(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\: \\bbox[5pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\, \\Big),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = a, \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}u(x,y)} = b, \\qquad\n{\\color{red}v(x,y)} = c, \\qquad \n{\\color{red}v(x,y)} = d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=x, \\qquad\ny=7x, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{3}{y^2}, \\qquad\nx=\\dfrac{5}{y^2}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=1,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=7,\\,x{y}^{2}=3,\\,x{y}^{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}$ and $v(x,y)={\\color{red}xy^2},$ we can define the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1} : (x,y) \\to \\left( \\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{y}{x}}\\,, \\: \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}xy^2} \\right).\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{T},$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v=x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv = u^2x^3\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^3 = \\dfrac{v}{u^2} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x = \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}$ back into $y=ux,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny =u\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}} = \\sqrt[3]{uv}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ can be given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T} : (u,v) \\to \\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{v}{u^2}}, \\, \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{ \\sqrt[3]{uv}} \\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198236 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6d98056-016e-520b-b752-d6a529f2c02d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "6d2cd5bf-899f-5b81-9512-ee98489d17ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-7322" :question/problem "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy} \\leq 4, \\: 2x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ and $v=xy.$ Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nLet's find the image of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le \\sqrt{xy}\\le 4,\\,2x\\le y\\le 3x} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,{2}^{2}\\le (\\sqrt{xy})^{2}\\le {4}^{2},\\,2\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 3} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le xy\\le 16,\\,2\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 3} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le v\\le 16,\\,2\\le u\\le 3} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,4\\le v\\le 16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $2 \\leq \\sqrt{xy}\\leq 4,$ we must have $x \\neq 0.$ Therefore, $u=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ is well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198237 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9156e571-2fe9-5347-95da-c1c97de1a3c9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Region Onto a Type I Region"}] :question/id #uuid "c78f087b-f0e5-58e6-a7a1-92a78c85c4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7323" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(x+y)^2 + y = 3$ and the line $y=-1.$\nConsider the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that  \n\n![](images/61/61a3c6677ac0c20f5cccc6808fa7b078f91c3ad784d59167d11a9829cd0f86c3.png)\n\nSubstituting $x+y = u$ and $y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1\\,⟹\\,v=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=-1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, -1).$\n\n![](images/84/84c624aab456b9a090c9c4e6d23c82d8e1f69425050b6f3ee9d4afd18e3f0cec.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\quad  -1 \\leq v \\leq 3-u^2 \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9156e571-2fe9-5347-95da-c1c97de1a3c9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Region Onto a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180d6391-e602-5efd-b15d-e264f73e267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371466} {:answer/id #uuid "cd37b28e-8d5e-5255-917c-1859931216cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,2\\le v\\le {u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371467} {:answer/id #uuid "535a2bbe-5d32-5952-b65c-d8072d0c6378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,{u}^{2}\\le v\\le 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371468} {:answer/id #uuid "71993bdd-313c-51a9-bb88-b7bdfe2cfb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,2\\le v\\le {u}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371469} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b6af6d-ea0b-52da-895e-fc324fcd6c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "180d6391-e602-5efd-b15d-e264f73e267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371466} :answer-field/id #uuid "73bf332d-e377-5a51-bb6e-8099852a01ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "748bffef-751d-5fc9-a40f-bab4bc7c7bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103964" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(x+{y}^{3})^{2}-y=1$ and the line $y=2.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+{y}^{3}$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+{y}^{3}=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+{y}^{3})^{2}-y=1\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=1\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-1 \\\\ v=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ v=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{3},2).$\n\n![](images/fc/fc6e896f50255deea574c52014935097b6c0b49aa6a1e54a9d6a484dc1b8cc61.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,{u}^{2}-1\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de3fa2fa-304c-583a-8c73-a5ff1db972e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Rectangular Region Onto a Given One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8b25d0f-d59c-57b3-a596-094a2fa26bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371922} {:answer/id #uuid "72883a1f-03e8-59d7-9c1e-67e9d5900697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371923} {:answer/id #uuid "76c28201-1240-5f72-87b6-49c31ffb6add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371924} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5d9b6c-780b-50a0-b459-dab290b52433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371925} {:answer/id #uuid "9774e3c6-8463-5669-9931-9ec714e68ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9774e3c6-8463-5669-9931-9ec714e68ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371926} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec49a288-bd93-5fe5-8569-21989622da29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec93a22b-8046-55ff-bd79-1effdf7c3586" :question/math-academy-id "q-161075" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{3}{y},\\,y=x,\\,y=\\frac{x}{3}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\,,\\,\\frac{x}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{3}{y},\\,y=x,\\,y=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}y=1,\\,{e}^{x}y=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={e}^{x}y$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,{e}^{x}y\\,,\\,\\frac{x}{y}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de3fa2fa-304c-583a-8c73-a5ff1db972e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Rectangular Region Onto a Given One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b34730-5f02-5098-b7fb-5232e115371e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371936} {:answer/id #uuid "8a25546c-4c65-59a2-ae10-fee6212f690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371937} {:answer/id #uuid "075c05d5-a6f6-5297-a443-b88b0dfc4257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371938} {:answer/id #uuid "e16013ed-3259-5a37-ac71-ba1971f865e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371939} {:answer/id #uuid "84d7448b-2caf-5eb4-9943-32338791b4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74b34730-5f02-5098-b7fb-5232e115371e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371936} :answer-field/id #uuid "38d2aefd-cac0-5e58-981d-4b55d8b67a4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21e23291-6559-5eea-9b70-7fd00996880f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161085" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\nx=y,\\,x=5y,\\,x=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}},\\,x=\\frac{7}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v},\\,\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=y,\\,x=5y,\\,x=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}},\\,x=\\frac{7}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=5,\\,x{y}^{2}=2,\\,x{y}^{2}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v=x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{3}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=u\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}=\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v},\\,\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{v}{u}}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9156e571-2fe9-5347-95da-c1c97de1a3c9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Region Onto a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b13425c2-ab49-5777-adaa-78d980364d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,u\\le v\\le -{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373376} {:answer/id #uuid "cecde188-fb5c-517a-9de9-bb3ac9180dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,{u}^{2}\\le v\\le u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373377} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a2b559-ca61-526e-a134-67a4f5978c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-{u}^{2}\\le v\\le u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373378} {:answer/id #uuid "10e40822-56d2-548b-aeb8-2c0c2915f59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-u\\le v\\le {u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373379} {:answer/id #uuid "4b01b496-b32b-5a33-9ce6-b2189e907548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,u\\le v\\le {u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b13425c2-ab49-5777-adaa-78d980364d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,u\\le v\\le -{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373376} :answer-field/id #uuid "23350df3-f523-5ba3-82a1-2c19d5fe91f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb3932ad-6398-5142-9ae7-9ead5c34792e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85314" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(x-y)^{2}+x+y=0$ and  the line $y=0.$ \nConsider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x-y$ and $v=x+y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x-y \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $x+y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x-y)^{2}+(x+y)=0\\,⟹\\,v=-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-y=u \\\\ x+y=v\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{v-u}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{v-u}{2}=0 & \\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to find the intersections of the resulting lines in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=u\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u(u+1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(-1,-1).$ \n\n![](images/05/05d7e65dca3c6d116d01be9e57fc83f3e3edcda4135a3f96fea82af8db4f2d1e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,u\\le v\\le -{u}^{2}}.\n$$"}]}
